WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.810
We'll now make a strange and
counterintuitive step.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:11.730
You know that human chromosomes are linear.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:16.720
But we want to think about them as
circular, cyclic chromosomes.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.090
Well, why would we do this?

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.849
Why would we distort the biological
reality?

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:27.113
The only reason is that it will make our
algorithmic problem much simpler

00:00:27.113 --> 00:00:32.310
while actually hardly affecting our
biological conclusion.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:37.854
Remember, I told you that analyzing
rearrangements in linear chromosomes is

00:00:37.854 --> 00:00:43.460
extremely difficult, even if we're dealing
with a unichromosomal genome.

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:47.401
Analyzing rearrangements in
multichromosomal

00:00:47.401 --> 00:00:50.950
linear genomes becomes nearly impossible.

00:00:51.375 --> 00:00:56.560
And, surprisingly enough, analyzing
rearrangements in multichromosomal

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:02.085
genomes with cyclic chromosomes will turn
out quite simple, we are about the

00:01:02.085 --> 00:01:07.690
discover it.
So now we will look at the cyclic genomes.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:10.755
In this case, cyclic genome, a genome with
a circle

00:01:10.755 --> 00:01:16.960
chromosome of consisting of two
chromosomes, and we will arrange blocks

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.710
in these chromosomes as directed black
edges.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:28.141
For example, +a, -b, -d, +c
will be arranged as four edges

00:01:28.141 --> 00:01:34.301
with corresponding orientation of these
edges along the circle.

00:01:34.301 --> 00:01:35.821
It's just a start.

00:01:35.821 --> 00:01:42.085
We will now add to these black edges
undirected red edges that connect

00:01:42.085 --> 00:01:46.866
adjacent synteny blocks.
So the two chromosomal

00:01:46.866 --> 00:01:51.940
genome will be represented as a graph
consisting of

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:57.200
two cycles.
These cycles, important, they have

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.240
edges of two colors, red and black.
Now what

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.240
do we do next?
First, let's notice that for every

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:11.640
genome there are many different ways to
visualize it.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:17.780
For example this is genome Q arranged in
natural order along the cycle, but we can

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:25.940
change arrangements of black edges of Q,
and how in this case this cycle looks like.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:30.440
Well, after +a, we should go to -b.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:32.670
That's what we do.
After -b,

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:35.430
you should go to -d, that's what we do.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:40.180
And after -d, we should go to +c,
and finally to +a.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:43.320
This is, this is the same genome and the

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.190
same graph in fact, the same cycle, just
visualized differently.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:56.670
Now let's try to figure out how a reversal
looks like in terms of these graphs.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.520
So, we start with genome P:

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:05.650
+a -b -c +d,
and let's make a reversal

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:12.370
on this genome, resulting with
+a -b -d and +c.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:18.060
How this new genome Q will look like in
the terms of our graph model.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:23.870
Well, after +a we should go to -b,
after -b we should go to -d

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:29.130
after -d we should go to +c
and finally from +c we should go to +a

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:34.900
So, therefore a transformation of genome P

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:42.230
into genome Q corresponds also to
transformations of the graph shown here.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:46.010
And how can we summarize what this
transformation does.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:49.230
Certainly it removes these

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:55.800
two red edges.
And it adds two new red edges.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:00.865
So in summary, a reversal essentially now
has geometric interpretation.

00:04:00.865 --> 00:04:08.180
It deletes two red edges and replaces them
by two other red edges on the same

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:14.590
four nodes.
Now, let's now talk about fission,

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:20.380
breaking a single chromosome into two.
In this case, a unichromosomal

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:26.750
genome P is broken into a genome with two
chromosomes: the genome Q.

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:30.410
So how does it look like in terms of our
graph?

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:33.936
So for, from +a, we should go to -b

00:04:33.936 --> 00:04:38.008
and from -b, we should go to +a,
so it will look like this.

00:04:38.008 --> 00:04:39.992
And also from -c

00:04:39.992 --> 00:04:46.704
we should go to +d
and from d we should go to c.

00:04:46.704 --> 00:04:49.577
So you see that geometrically also a

00:04:49.577 --> 00:04:56.060
single cycle is broken into two cycles.
What does it mean geometrically again?

00:04:56.060 --> 00:05:02.799
Once again, we remove two red edges and
add two new red edges.

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:04.940
And similarly,

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:07.990
in the case of fusion, the reverse
operation, we would

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:13.580
also remove two red edges and add two new
red edges.

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:19.630
And therefore, we define the notion of
"2-break", a rearrangement that replaces

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:25.610
two red edges with two new red edges on
the same four nodes.

00:05:25.610 --> 00:05:30.810
What we have just accomplished, instead of
four different operations,

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:34.810
reversals, translocations, fusions and
fissions,

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:37.530
we have a single operation, a 2-break.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:43.381
I describe how to model reversals,
fusions, and fissions as 2-breaks.

00:05:43.381 --> 00:05:44.851
What about translocations.

00:05:44.851 --> 00:05:49.805
It's the same story.
Here are two linear chromosomes that after

00:05:49.805 --> 00:05:56.439
translocations transform into a blue
chromosome and a green chromosome

00:05:56.439 --> 00:05:57.716
shown here.

00:05:57.716 --> 00:06:04.925
Once again we represent them as black-red
paths.

00:06:04.925 --> 00:06:10.330
Let's circularize them by just gluing the
ends using one of the red edges.

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:17.380
And after we circularize them, let's
perform this 2-break.

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:21.950
We'll choose these two edges.
We'll remove them and substitute

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:25.290
them by other two red edges.

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:29.530
And afterwards, we will simply remove the
edges that we used

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:31.850
for circularization, like here, which

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:35.130
is essentially linearization of circular
chromosomes.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:37.060
So if you look at this, then you

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:42.039
see that we essentially mimic
translocations using our graphs.

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:47.390
And we will define 2-break distance as the
minimum number

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:52.020
of 2-breaks transforming one genome into
another.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:56.475
And 2-break distance problem:
input two genomes on the same set

00:06:56.475 --> 00:07:02.740
of synteny blocks, and output the 2-break
distance between these genomes.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:07.510
It's now time to solve this problem.
But before we do this, we need to

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:12.980
introduce the key notion in studies of
genome rearrangements which is called

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:14.460
the breakpoint graphs.

